      "rationale": "The bug is a single-character flip (`!=` vs `==`) in `group_as`'s `valid_prev` check against the keyword `NULL`: currently it allows any non-NULL keyword to be grouped with a following `AS ...` into an alias Identifier (breaking \"dont alias keywords\"), while specifically rejecting NULL itself (breaking \"null with as\"). The expected values come directly from the CHANGELOG entries documenting that NULL must be alias-able via AS and that plain keywords must not be, plus tracing `_group`'s prev/next matching logic in grouping.py to confirm the resulting token structure for each case. Both tests fail on the current buggy comparison and pass once it is corrected to `==`, without affecting any other grouping behaviour.",
